From 1c48dede572be3450ff8678a5e2e5afbf8978115 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:27:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] scheduler: keep recent builds and serve them as
 Events.RecentBuilds

A watcher knows only what finished since it connected. A reload, a
second tab or a failover leaves a build list empty, and finished builds
pass through the event stream once. The scheduler now remembers them.

It keeps two rings of shared, immutable records next to the counters:
the last 10,000 builds that finished on a builder, without error text,
and the last 2,000 failures, with the masked text DrvGone carries. A
record names the derivation, its system, the builder, the outcome and
status, the assignment, when it was assigned, started and finished,
and up to 4 identities of the clients that waited on it. Cache hits and
queued drops stay out. A new term clears both rings, as it clears the
counters.

Events.RecentBuilds returns them newest first, in batches of 256: up to
1,000 records older than before_ms, up to 256 failures with their text,
or the newest record of up to 16 named derivations. The last batch says
whether the ring holds anything older. A call copies pointers under the
dispatcher's lock and serializes after releasing it. It authorizes as
Watch does, takes at most 4 calls per caller and 16 at once, and
answers UNAVAILABLE on a passive node. A new gauge counts what each
ring holds.

events-test covers the caps, the text only in the failures ring, what
stays out, and a new term. events-grpc-test covers paging, the failures
ring, named derivations, anonymous callers, the per-caller cap and a
passive node.
